The '''Cabinet Office''' ({{Nihongo|内閣府|Naikaku-fu}}) is a [[Cabinet of Okaiken|cabinet-level]] administrative organ of the [[Government of Okaiken]] that serves as the primary coordinating body of the [[Executive (government)|executive branch]]. Headed by the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] in his capacity as Minister of State for the Cabinet Office, it is responsible for cross-ministerial policy coordination, the formulation of key economic and fiscal policy, disaster management oversight, and the administration of several important commissions and agencies, including the [[Cabinet Secretariat (Okaiken)|Cabinet Secretariat]], the [[National Public Safety Commission (Okaiken)|National Public Safety Commission]], and the [[Council for Gender Equality (Okaiken)|Council for Gender Equality]].

Established on 6 January 2001 as part of the [[2001 Central Government Reform (Okaiken)|2001 Central Government Reform]], the Cabinet Office was formed through the merger of the [[Prime Minister's Office (Okaiken)|Prime Minister's Office]] ({{Nihongo|総理府|Sōri-fu}}), the [[Economic Planning Agency (Okaiken)|Economic Planning Agency]] ({{Nihongo|経済企画庁|Keizai Kikaku-chō}}), and the [[Okinawa Development Agency (Okaiken)|Okinawa Development Agency]] ({{Nihongo|沖縄開発庁|Okinawa Kaihatsu-chō}}). The reform was designed to strengthen the Prime Minister's capacity for policy leadership and to overcome the {{Nihongo|"sectionalism"|縦割り行政|tatewari gyōsei}} — the tendency of individual ministries to operate in silos — that had long characterised Sanese bureaucratic governance.

With a budget of approximately {{currency|32 billion|SEN}} in FY 2025 (roughly 2% of total government expenditure of {{currency|1.6 trillion|SEN}}) and approximately 3,850 personnel, the Cabinet Office is among the smaller central government organs in terms of staff, but its coordinating authority and proximity to the Prime Minister give it outsized influence over national policy. It houses the [[Chief Cabinet Secretary (Okaiken)|Chief Cabinet Secretary]] — often described as the government's primary spokesperson and the second most powerful figure in the executive — and oversees the [[National Public Safety Commission (Okaiken)|National Public Safety Commission]], which exercises administrative oversight of the [[Okaiken National Police Agency|National Police Agency]].

Under the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]], the Cabinet Office has assumed expanded responsibilities, including the coordination of the {{Nihongo|[[Yoshida Threshold]]|吉田閾値|Yoshida Shiki-ichi}} (the binding 40% women in cabinet requirement), the establishment of the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] by executive order, the initiation of the [[Kizan prosecution]], and the coordination of the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]] through the legislative process. The creation of the dedicated [[Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability (Okaiken)|Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability]] portfolio — held by [[Shimane Yoshida]] of the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]] — represents the most significant structural expansion of the Cabinet Office's ministerial complement since the 2001 reform.

==History==

===Pre-reform era (1947–2000)===
Prior to the 2001 reform, the functions now performed by the Cabinet Office were distributed across several agencies. The [[Prime Minister's Office (Okaiken)|Prime Minister's Office]] ({{Nihongo|総理府|Sōri-fu}}) served as the administrative arm of the Prime Minister, but its coordinating authority was limited by the absence of statutory mechanisms to compel other ministries to comply with its directives. The [[Economic Planning Agency (Okaiken)|Economic Planning Agency]] ({{Nihongo|経済企画庁|Keizai Kikaku-chō}}) produced economic forecasts and formulated medium-term plans, but lacked implementation authority. The [[Okinawa Development Agency (Okaiken)|Okinawa Development Agency]] ({{Nihongo|沖縄開発庁|Okinawa Kaihatsu-chō}}) managed the special development programmes for the [[Okinawa Prefecture|Okinawa]] region.

Throughout the post-war period, the structural weakness of the Prime Minister's coordinating role was a recurring theme in Sanese governance. The {{Nihongo|"sectionalism"|縦割り行政|tatewari gyōsei}} of the bureaucracy — in which each ministry operated as a virtually autonomous fiefdom — meant that cross-cutting policy issues such as disaster management, economic coordination, and science and technology policy often fell between institutional cracks. The {{Nihongo|Administrative Reform Council|行政改革会議|Gyōsei Kaikaku Kaigi}}, established in 1997 under the [[Aoyagi administration (Okaiken)|Aoyagi administration's]] predecessor, identified the creation of a strengthened Cabinet Office as the centrepiece of its reform recommendations.

===2001 Central Government Reform===
{{Main|2001 Central Government Reform (Okaiken)}}
The [[2001 Central Government Reform (Okaiken)|2001 Central Government Reform]] ({{Nihongo|中央省庁再編|Chūō Shōchō Saihen}}) was the most significant restructuring of the Sanese bureaucracy since the post-war occupation. The Cabinet Office was established on 6 January 2001 as the principal organ for policy coordination and planning, with the Prime Minister designated as its head. The reform merged the Prime Minister's Office, the Economic Planning Agency, and the Okinawa Development Agency into the new entity, and transferred several important policy councils — including the [[Economic and Fiscal Policy Council (Okaiken)|Economic and Fiscal Policy Council]] and the [[Council for Science and Technology Policy (Okaiken)|Council for Science and Technology Policy]] — to the Cabinet Office's jurisdiction.

The reform legislation, principally the {{Nihongo|Cabinet Office Establishment Act|内閣府設置法|Naikaku-fu Secchi Hō}}, granted the Cabinet Office three key powers that its predecessor agencies had lacked: the authority to formulate basic policies on cross-ministerial matters; the power to coordinate and, in certain circumstances, direct the work of other ministries; and the mandate to establish and operate policy councils chaired by the Prime Minister. These powers were intended to transform the Prime Minister from what critics had called a {{Nihongo|"coalition manager"|連立管理者|renrui kanrisha}} into a genuine policy leader.

However, the reform's effectiveness was debated from the outset. Critics argued that the Cabinet Office's coordinating authority remained dependent on the Prime Minister's political capital and that, in the absence of strong personal leadership, the traditional {{Nihongo|"bureaucratic supremacy"|官僚支配|kanryō shihai}} of the line ministries would reassert itself. The early years of the Cabinet Office's existence were characterised by a gradual expansion of its staff and responsibilities, but its actual influence fluctuated significantly depending on the political strength and policy engagement of the incumbent Prime Minister.

===Modern era (2001–present)===
The Cabinet Office's role has evolved considerably over the two and a half decades since its establishment. The [[Aoyagi administration (Okaiken)|Aoyagi administration]] (2014–2021) made extensive use of the Cabinet Office's policy councils, particularly the Economic and Fiscal Policy Council, to drive its agenda on wealth taxation and social investment. The administration's controversial {{Nihongo|"Wealth Solidarity Surcharge"|富の連帯追加税|Tomi no Rendai Tsuikazei}} was formulated through the Council's deliberations, and the Cabinet Office's {{Nihongo|Gender Equality Bureau|男女共同参画局|Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Kyoku}} played a central role in advancing the administration's gender equality agenda.

The [[Honda Shigetsuma|Honda]] premiership (2021–2026) saw a shift in the Cabinet Office's orientation. Prime Minister Honda, a firm believer in market-oriented reform, used the Cabinet Office primarily as a vehicle for his digital modernisation agenda, establishing the [[Digital Agency (Okaiken)|Digital Agency]] in 2021 as a cabinet-level body under the Cabinet Office's administrative umbrella. However, the Honda years also exposed the Cabinet Office's limitations: the {{Nihongo|"En Crisis"|円危機|En Kiki}} of January–February 2026 and the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026 both revealed critical coordination failures, with the Chief Cabinet Secretary and the National Public Safety Commission struggling to manage the government's response to cascading crises.

The [[Ikazaki Cabinet]], formed on 20 May 2026, has significantly expanded the Cabinet Office's policy portfolio. The establishment of the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] by executive order on the first day of the administration, the initiation of the [[Kizan prosecution]], and the coordination of the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]] through the Diet have all been managed through the Cabinet Office. The creation of the dedicated [[Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability (Okaiken)|Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability]] portfolio — a position that combines the Cabinet Office's existing gender equality functions with a new mandate for democratic institutional reform — represents the most significant structural expansion of the Office's ministerial complement since 2001.

==Organization==

The Cabinet Office is organised into the Minister's Secretariat, the Cabinet Secretariat, several policy bureaus, and a number of affiliated councils and agencies. Unlike the line ministries, which are headed by a single minister, the Cabinet Office houses multiple ministers of state, each responsible for a specific policy area.

===Minister's Secretariat===
The {{Nihongo|'''Minister's Secretariat'''|大臣官房|Daijin Kanbō}} is the administrative backbone of the Cabinet Office, responsible for overall coordination of the Office's operations, personnel management, budget formulation, public relations, and information systems. It oversees the work of the various policy bureaus and serves as the primary interface between the Cabinet Office and the other ministries and agencies. The Secretariat is headed by the {{Nihongo|Deputy Director-General|次長|Jichō}}, a senior career bureaucrat who serves as the highest-ranking official in the Cabinet Office's administrative hierarchy.

The Minister's Secretariat also includes the {{Nihongo|Public Relations Office|広報室|Kōhō-shitsu}}, which manages the government's official communications, and the {{Nihongo|Cabinet Intelligence Office|内閣情報室|Naikaku Jōhō-shitsu}}, which coordinates intelligence gathering and analysis across the government.

===Cabinet Secretariat===
{{Main|Cabinet Secretariat (Okaiken)}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Cabinet Secretariat'''|内閣官房|Naikaku Kanbō}} is the operational centre of the Cabinet, responsible for coordinating the Cabinet's agenda, preparing materials for Cabinet meetings, managing the flow of information between the Prime Minister and the ministries, and overseeing the implementation of Cabinet decisions. It is headed by the [[Chief Cabinet Secretary (Okaiken)|Chief Cabinet Secretary]] ({{Nihongo|内閣官房長官|Naikaku Kanbō Chōkan}}), who serves as the government's primary spokesperson, the Prime Minister's chief of staff, and the de facto second most powerful figure in the executive.

The Cabinet Secretariat comprises three key offices:
* {{Nihongo|'''Cabinet Affairs Office'''|内閣参事官室|Naikaku Sanjikan-shitsu}}: Handles the preparation and coordination of Cabinet meeting materials and decisions.
* {{Nihongo|'''Cabinet Information Research Office'''|内閣情報調査室|Naikaku Jōhō Chōsa-shitsu}}: Conducts intelligence analysis and provides security briefings to the Prime Minister and the Cabinet.
* {{Nihongo|'''Cabinet Public Relations Office'''|内閣広報室|Naikaku Kōhō-shitsu}}: Manages government communications and media relations.

Under the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]], the Cabinet Secretariat was criticised for its handling of the [[Satori AI Blackout]] response, with Chief Cabinet Secretary [[Minoru Kihara]] accused of delaying the government's emergency declaration by several hours. The current Chief Cabinet Secretary, [[Tomoko Aoki]] of the [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]], has pledged to reform the Secretariat's crisis response protocols and to strengthen the Cabinet Information Research Office's capacity for real-time situational awareness.

===Policy bureaus===
The Cabinet Office houses several policy bureaus responsible for specific cross-cutting policy areas:

;{{Nihongo|Gender Equality Bureau|男女共同参画局|Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Kyoku}}
:Responsible for the formulation and coordination of policies on gender equality, women's empowerment, and the elimination of gender-based discrimination. The Bureau serves as the secretariat for the [[Council for Gender Equality (Okaiken)|Council for Gender Equality]] and oversees the implementation of the {{Nihongo|Basic Act for Gender Equal Society|男女共同参画社会基本法|Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Shakai Kihon Hō}}. Under the Ikazaki Cabinet, the Bureau has been significantly expanded to support the work of the [[Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability (Okaiken)|Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability]], [[Shimane Yoshida]], including the administration of the [[Yoshida Threshold]].

;{{Nihongo|Disaster Management Bureau|防災局|Bōsai-kyoku}}
:Responsible for the formulation of the Basic Disaster Management Plan, coordination of disaster response and recovery operations, and oversight of the {{Nihongo|Disaster Management Council|中央防災会議|Chūō Bōsai Kaigi}}. The Bureau coordinates the government's response to major natural disasters and manages the emergency communication systems that link central and local government agencies.

;{{Nihongo|Economic and Social Research Institute|経済社会総合研究所|Keizai Shakai Sōgō Kenkyūjo}}
:Conducts economic research, publishes economic forecasts and statistical analyses, and provides analytical support for the [[Economic and Fiscal Policy Council (Okaiken)|Economic and Fiscal Policy Council]]. The Institute produces the annual {{Nihongo|Economic and Fiscal Projections|経済財政展望|Keizai Zaisei Tenbō}}, which serves as the baseline for the government's fiscal planning.

;{{Nihongo|Regional Revitalisation Bureau|地方創生局|Chihō Sōsei-kyoku}}
:Coordinates policies to address regional economic disparities, depopulation in rural areas, and the concentration of population and economic activity in the [[Okami Metropolis|Okami]] metropolitan region. The Bureau administers the {{Nihongo|Regional Revitalisation Fund|地方創生基金|Chihō Sōsei Kikin}} and works with local governments on development strategies.

==Key agencies and commissions==

===National Public Safety Commission===
{{Main|National Public Safety Commission (Okaiken)}}
The {{Nihongo|'''National Public Safety Commission'''|国家公安委員会|Kokka Kōan Iinkai}} is a cabinet-level commission housed within the Cabinet Office that exercises administrative oversight of the [[Okaiken National Police Agency|National Police Agency]] ({{Nihongo|警察庁|Keisatsu-chō}}) and the [[Okaiken Coast Guard|Coast Guard]]. The Commission is composed of a chairman, who is a minister of state, and five commissioners appointed by the Prime Minister with the consent of the [[Diet of Okaiken|National Diet]]. The Commission's statutory mandate is to ensure the democratic and politically neutral administration of police functions.

The Commission has been at the centre of several recent controversies. Under the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]], Chairman [[Tsubasa Amami]] (PPO) was criticised for the National Police Agency's handling of the {{Nihongo|"Mirror Yoshi"|鏡よし|Kagami Yoshi}} protests following the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]], with allegations that police used excessive force against demonstrators. Amami was defeated in the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]] by [[Ueno Naoya]] (SCP) in the [[Hirogawa Prefecture|Hirogawa]] 3rd district. The current chairman, [[Shoji Yamashita]] (SCP), has announced a review of police crowd control protocols and a strengthening of the Commission's civilian oversight mechanisms.

===Economic and Fiscal Policy Council===
{{Main|Economic and Fiscal Policy Council (Okaiken)}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Economic and Fiscal Policy Council'''|経済財政諮問会議|Keizai Zaisei Shimon Kaigi}} is the most influential of the policy councils housed within the Cabinet Office. Chaired by the Prime Minister, it is responsible for the formulation of basic policies on economic and fiscal management, the coordination of the government's economic strategy, and the review of the national budget framework before it is submitted to the Diet. The Council's deliberations are informed by the research of the [[Economic and Social Research Institute (Okaiken)|Economic and Social Research Institute]] and by input from the [[Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)|Ministry of Finance]] and the [[Bank of Okaiken]].

The Council played a central role in the government's response to the {{Nihongo|"En Crisis"|円危機|En Kiki}} of January–February 2026, formulating the fiscal consolidation package that Finance Minister [[Satsuki Katayama]] announced in late February. Under the Ikazaki Cabinet, the Council has been tasked with developing a comprehensive fiscal resilience strategy in response to the [[ACTA]] credit downgrade from AAA to AA in May 2026.

===Council for Gender Equality===
{{Main|Council for Gender Equality (Okaiken)}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Council for Gender Equality'''|男女共同参画会議|Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Kaigi}} is a policy council chaired by the Prime Minister that is responsible for the formulation of basic policies on gender equality and the monitoring of their implementation. The Council was established under the {{Nihongo|Basic Act for Gender Equal Society|男女共同参画社会基本法|Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Shakai Kihon Hō}} of 1999 and has been housed within the Cabinet Office since the 2001 reform.

Under the Ikazaki Cabinet, the Council has assumed heightened importance as the institutional home of the [[Yoshida Threshold]] — the binding requirement that at least 40% of cabinet positions be held by women. The Council's secretariat, the Gender Equality Bureau, has been expanded to support the work of the new [[Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability (Okaiken)|Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability]], [[Shimane Yoshida]], who chairs the Council's working groups on democratic accountability and institutional reform.

===National Space Development Agency===
{{Main|National Space Development Agency (Okaiken)}}
The {{Nihongo|'''National Space Development Agency'''|宇宙開発機構|Uchū Kaihatsu Kikō}} (NSDA) is an independent administrative agency under the Cabinet Office's jurisdiction, responsible for the planning and implementation of Okaiken's space development programme, including satellite launches, space exploration, and the development of space-based technologies. The NSDA operates the [[Okami Space Center]] and manages Okaiken's contributions to international space cooperation programmes.

The NSDA's budget of approximately {{currency|180 billion|SEN}} (FY 2025) is drawn from the Cabinet Office's allocation, making it the single largest line item within the Office's budget. The Agency has been at the forefront of Okaiken's satellite navigation and earth observation programmes, and its work on the {{Nihongo|"Hikari"|光|Hikari}} series of communication satellites has been critical to the nation's digital infrastructure.

===Other affiliated bodies===
The Cabinet Office also houses or provides administrative support to several other important bodies:
* '''[[Council for Science and Technology Policy (Okaiken)|Council for Science and Technology Policy]]''' ({{Nihongo|総合科学技術会議|Sōgō Kagaku Gijutsu Kaigi}}): Advises the Prime Minister on science and technology policy and coordinates cross-ministerial R&D investment.
* '''[[Central Disaster Management Council (Okaiken)|Central Disaster Management Council]]''' ({{Nihongo|中央防災会議|Chūō Bōsai Kaigi}}): Formulates the Basic Disaster Management Plan and coordinates the government's disaster preparedness and response.
* '''[[Financial Council (Okaiken)|Financial Council]]''' ({{Nihongo|金融会議|Kin'yū Kaigi}}): Coordinates the government's response to financial crises and advises on the stability of the financial system.
* '''[[Personal Information Protection Commission (Okaiken)|Personal Information Protection Commission]]''' ({{Nihongo|個人情報保護委員会|Kojin Jōhō Hogo Iinkai}}): Oversees the implementation of data protection legislation and investigates data breaches.

==Key policy areas==

===Cross-ministerial policy coordination===
The Cabinet Office's primary function is to coordinate policy across the line ministries, overcoming the {{Nihongo|"sectionalism"|縦割り行政|tatewari gyōsei}} that has historically characterised Sanese bureaucratic governance. This coordination function is exercised through the Cabinet Secretariat, which manages the flow of information and decisions between the Prime Minister and the ministries, and through the various policy councils, which provide forums for inter-ministerial deliberation chaired by the Prime Minister or a designated minister.

In practice, the effectiveness of the Cabinet Office's coordinating role has varied significantly depending on the political strength and policy engagement of the incumbent Prime Minister. During periods of strong prime ministerial leadership — such as the early years of the Aoyagi administration — the Cabinet Office has been able to drive ambitious cross-cutting agendas. During periods of weaker leadership, the line ministries have tended to reassert their autonomy, reducing the Cabinet Office to a more passive role.

===Economic and fiscal policy===
The Cabinet Office, through the [[Economic and Fiscal Policy Council (Okaiken)|Economic and Fiscal Policy Council]], plays a central role in the formulation of the government's economic and fiscal strategy. The Council reviews the national budget framework before it is submitted to the Diet, sets medium-term fiscal targets, and coordinates the government's response to economic crises. The Council's deliberations are informed by the research of the [[Economic and Social Research Institute (Okaiken)|Economic and Social Research Institute]], which produces the annual economic and fiscal projections that serve as the baseline for fiscal planning.

The Cabinet Office's economic policy role was particularly prominent during the {{Nihongo|"En Crisis"|円危機|En Kiki}} of January–February 2026, when the Council formulated the emergency fiscal consolidation package that stabilised the currency. Under the Ikazaki Cabinet, the Council has been tasked with developing a comprehensive fiscal resilience strategy in response to the [[ACTA]] credit downgrade.

===Disaster management===
The Cabinet Office's {{Nihongo|Disaster Management Bureau|防災局|Bōsai-kyoku}} is responsible for the formulation of the {{Nihongo|Basic Disaster Management Plan|防災基本計画|Bōsai Kihon Keikaku}}, the coordination of disaster response and recovery operations, and the management of the emergency communication systems that link central and local government agencies. The Bureau serves as the secretariat for the [[Central Disaster Management Council (Okaiken)|Central Disaster Management Council]], chaired by the Prime Minister, which is the government's highest-level disaster management body.

The Cabinet Office's disaster management functions have been tested repeatedly in recent years, most notably during the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026, which overwhelmed the government's existing crisis response protocols. The Blackout exposed critical weaknesses in the Cabinet Office's crisis management framework, including inadequate real-time situational awareness, insufficient coordination between the Cabinet Secretariat and the line ministries, and the absence of analogue backup systems for critical communication channels.

===Gender equality and democratic accountability===
The Cabinet Office's gender equality functions, administered through the {{Nihongo|Gender Equality Bureau|男女共同参画局|Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Kyoku}} and the [[Council for Gender Equality (Okaiken)|Council for Gender Equality]], have assumed significantly greater prominence under the Ikazaki Cabinet. The [[Yoshida Threshold]] — the binding requirement that at least 40% of cabinet positions be held by women — is administered through the Council, and the new [[Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability (Okaiken)|Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability]] portfolio combines the Bureau's existing gender equality mandate with a new responsibility for democratic institutional reform.

The creation of the combined portfolio reflects the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]]'s position that democratic accountability is a gendered issue — that the underrepresentation of women in governance is itself a democratic deficit — and has been described by political commentators as one of the most innovative institutional reforms of the Ikazaki Cabinet.

==Key legislation==

The Cabinet Office operates under a framework of legislation that defines its mandate, powers, and organisational structure. Key statutes include:

* '''[[Cabinet Law (Okaiken)|Cabinet Law]]''' ({{Nihongo|内閣法|Naikaku Hō}}, 1947): Establishes the Cabinet as the executive organ of government, defines the collective responsibility of ministers, and sets out the procedures for Cabinet decision-making. The Cabinet Law provides the legal basis for the Cabinet Office's coordinating role and for the Prime Minister's authority to establish policy councils.
* '''[[National Government Organization Act (Okaiken)|National Government Organization Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|国家行政組織法|Kokka Gyōsei Soshiki Hō}}, 1948): Establishes the framework for the organisation of the central government, including the creation, abolition, and reorganisation of ministries and agencies. The 2001 amendment to this Act provided the legal basis for the establishment of the Cabinet Office.
* '''[[Cabinet Office Establishment Act (Okaiken)|Cabinet Office Establishment Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|内閣府設置法|Naikaku-fu Secchi Hō}}, 2001): Defines the Cabinet Office's mandate, organisational structure, and powers. The Act designates the Prime Minister as the head of the Cabinet Office and establishes the various policy councils and agencies under the Office's jurisdiction.
* '''[[Basic Act for Gender Equal Society (Okaiken)|Basic Act for Gender Equal Society]]''' ({{Nihongo|男女共同参画社会基本法|Danjo Kyōdō Sankyaku Shakai Kihon Hō}}, 1999): Establishes the basic principles of gender equality policy and provides the legal basis for the [[Council for Gender Equality (Okaiken)|Council for Gender Equality]] and the Gender Equality Bureau.
* '''[[Disaster Countermeasures Basic Act (Okaiken)|Disaster Countermeasures Basic Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|災害対策基本法|Saigai Taisaku Kihon Hō}}, 1961): Establishes the framework for disaster management, including the roles of the Central Disaster Management Council and the Disaster Management Bureau.
* '''[[National Police Act (Okaiken)|National Police Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|警察法|Keisatsu Hō}}, 1954): Establishes the [[National Public Safety Commission (Okaiken)|National Public Safety Commission]] and defines its oversight role over the National Police Agency.

==Recent events==

===The Yoshida Threshold===
{{Main|Yoshida Threshold}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Yoshida Threshold'''|吉田閾値|Yoshida Shiki-ichi}} is a binding coalition condition, extracted by [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]] leader [[Shimane Yoshida]] during the {{Nihongo|"Three-Day War"|三日間の戦争|Mikkakan no Sensō}} coalition negotiations in May 2026, requiring that at least 40% of all cabinet positions be held by women. The Threshold is codified in the coalition agreement as a formal condition of SDP participation, meaning that any cabinet reshuffle that falls below 40% women would constitute a breach of the coalition and could trigger the SDP's withdrawal from government.

The Threshold is administered through the Cabinet Office's [[Council for Gender Equality (Okaiken)|Council for Gender Equality]] and its secretariat, the Gender Equality Bureau. The current cabinet exceeds the threshold at 47.1% (8 of 17 ministers), the highest proportion of women in any cabinet in Sanese history. The Threshold has been described as the most significant institutional reform in gender equality policy since the passage of the [[Basic Act for Gender Equal Society (Okaiken)|Basic Act for Gender Equal Society]] in 1999.

===The Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission===
{{Main|Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission'''|山本日奈安全委員会|Yamamoto Hina Anzen Iinkai}} was established by executive order on 20 May 2026 — the first day of the Ikazaki Cabinet — as an independent rail safety body named after the 17-year-old student killed in the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]]. The Commission has the unprecedented authority to ground any rail service in Okaiken without ministerial approval if it determines that staffing levels or safety protocols are insufficient.

The establishment of the Commission was coordinated through the Cabinet Office, with the executive order signed by Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] in his capacity as Minister of State for the Cabinet Office. Transport Minister [[Seiran Mochizuki]] (OSP) was given direct oversight of the Commission's implementation. The Commission's creation marked the first time that an independent safety body had been granted grounding authority without ministerial approval in Sanese transport governance.

===The Kizan prosecution===
{{Main|Kizan prosecution}}
The criminal prosecution of [[Kizan Heavy Industries]], the private contractor whose maintenance failures were identified as the proximate cause of the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]], was initiated from the Cabinet Office on 22 May 2026. The prosecution — formally pursued by Justice Minister [[Hiroshi Nakajima]] — marked the first criminal case against a private infrastructure contractor in Sanese history.

The decision to pursue criminal prosecution was coordinated through the Cabinet Office, with the Chief Cabinet Secretary's office managing the inter-ministerial coordination between the Ministry of Justice, the National Police Agency (under the oversight of the National Public Safety Commission), and the Cabinet Secretariat. The prosecution has been described as a defining moment in the Ikazaki Cabinet's approach to corporate accountability.

===The Two-Man Mandate===
The {{Nihongo|'''Two-Man Mandate'''|二人乗務義務|Futari Nōmu Gimu}} — a core [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] demand during the coalition negotiations — requires all conventional rail services in Okaiken to operate with a minimum of two crew members. The mandate reversed the Honda cabinet's one-man operation policy that had been identified as a contributing factor in the Nakayama Station incident. Implementation oversight was assigned to Deputy Prime Minister [[Rento Nakayama]] in his capacity as Minister of Social Affairs, with Transport Minister Mochizuki responsible for operational enforcement. The coordination of the mandate's implementation was managed through the Cabinet Office.

===Digital Sovereignty Act coordination===
{{Main|Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)}}
The [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]] ({{Nihongo|デジタル主権法|Dejitaru Shuken Hō}}), the flagship legislation of the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]], was coordinated through the Cabinet Office during its passage through the [[Diet of Okaiken|National Diet]]. The Act, which mandates that all critical national infrastructure maintain analogue backup systems and that no single AI system may control more than 40% of any essential service grid, was a direct response to the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]].

The Cabinet Office's coordinating role was essential to the Act's passage, as the legislation required the alignment of multiple ministries — including the [[Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (Okaiken)|Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry]], the [[Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications (Okaiken)|Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications]], and the [[Digital Agency (Okaiken)|Digital Agency]] — behind a single legislative framework. The Chief Cabinet Secretary's office managed the inter-ministerial coordination, while the [[Minister for Digital Sovereignty (Okaiken)|Minister for Digital Sovereignty]], [[Shizuka Ono]] (SDP), was appointed to oversee implementation.

==Controversies==

===Chief Cabinet Secretary's role in the Blackout response===
The most significant controversy involving the Cabinet Office in recent years concerns the role of Chief Cabinet Secretary [[Minoru Kihara]] in the government's response to the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of 1–4 May 2026. The Blackout, which paralysed the nation for 72 hours and caused 23 deaths and an estimated {{currency|420 billion|SEN}} in economic damage, exposed critical failures in the Cabinet Secretariat's crisis management protocols.

A subsequent investigation by the [[Diet of Okaiken|Diet's]] {{Nihongo|Special Committee on the Satori Crisis|サトリ危機特別委員会|Satori Kiki Tokubetsu Iinkai} found that Kihara had delayed the government's emergency declaration by approximately three hours on the night of 1 May, during which time the Cabinet Secretariat's crisis response team was unable to establish reliable communication with the affected ministries. The investigation revealed that the Cabinet Secretariat's emergency communication systems — which relied on the same [[Satori AI]] infrastructure that had failed — were non-functional for the first six hours of the crisis, and that no adequate analogue backup existed.

The committee's report, published in June 2026, concluded that "the Cabinet Secretariat's crisis response framework was fundamentally compromised by its reliance on the very digital infrastructure whose failure constituted the emergency." The report recommended the establishment of dedicated analogue communication channels for the Cabinet Secretariat, the creation of a standing crisis management cell within the Cabinet Office, and the strengthening of the Cabinet Information Research Office's capacity for independent situational assessment.

Kihara defended his actions, arguing that the delay in the emergency declaration was necessitated by the need to verify conflicting reports from the field and that the Cabinet Secretariat had operated within the constraints of the available information. However, the controversy contributed to the PPO's decisive defeat in the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]], and Kihara's handling of the crisis has been widely cited as a case study in the dangers of over-reliance on digital infrastructure for critical government functions.

===National Public Safety Commission and the Mirror Yoshi protests===
Under Chairman [[Tsubasa Amami]] (PPO), the National Public Safety Commission faced sustained criticism over the National Police Agency's handling of the {{Nihongo|"Mirror Yoshi"|鏡よし|Kagami Yoshi}} protests that followed the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] in March 2026. Allegations that police used excessive force — including the deployment of water cannons and tear gas against predominantly peaceful demonstrators in [[Okami Metropolis|Okami]] and [[Sakai]] — led to formal complaints to the Commission and calls for Amami's resignation.

The Commission's internal review, conducted under Amami's chairmanship, concluded that the police response had been "proportionate and necessary," a finding that was rejected by the [[Okaiken Bar Association]] and by several prefectural human rights commissions. The controversy highlighted the structural tension inherent in the Commission's design: while it is statutorily mandated to ensure the democratic and politically neutral administration of police functions, its chairman is a political appointee who serves at the Prime Minister's pleasure, creating potential conflicts of interest when police actions are directed against protests targeting the government itself.

Under the Ikazaki Cabinet, Chairman [[Shoji Yamashita]] (SCP) has announced a comprehensive review of the Commission's oversight mechanisms, including the establishment of an independent civilian complaints body and the revision of police crowd control protocols.

